1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,400
April the second.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:04,600
Meera has found her cart.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:08,640
Wheels, a stove, kettles, a stack of glasses.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,600
The price?

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,040
Thirty-six thousand rupees.

6
00:00:13,188 --> 00:00:16,468
Thirty-six thousand rupees, out of the galla.

7
00:00:17,076 --> 00:00:19,348
So did Meera just lose thirty-six thousand

8
00:00:19,428 --> 00:00:20,228
rupees?

9
00:00:26,089 --> 00:00:28,449
First, last lesson's answers.

10
00:00:28,729 --> 00:00:29,049
One.

11
00:00:29,449 --> 00:00:31,689
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

12
00:00:31,769 --> 00:00:34,129
of milk, and Meera will pay later.

13
00:00:34,409 --> 00:00:36,329
The stall now has eight thousand rupees

14
00:00:36,409 --> 00:00:39,209
of stock — milk and supplies to sell.

15
00:00:39,289 --> 00:00:40,289
That's an asset.

16
00:00:41,089 --> 00:00:43,809
And it owes Gopal eight thousand rupees.

17
00:00:43,889 --> 00:00:45,089
That's a liability.

18
00:00:45,553 --> 00:00:45,953
Two.

19
00:00:46,433 --> 00:00:47,873
Meera's personal scooter?

20
00:00:48,353 --> 00:00:49,873
Not an asset of the stall.

21
00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:50,953
It's hers.

22
00:00:51,153 --> 00:00:53,313
The stall is its own person.

23
00:00:53,759 --> 00:00:54,399
Three.

24
00:00:54,639 --> 00:00:57,119
The stall has eighty thousand, and owes

25
00:00:57,279 --> 00:00:58,399
thirty thousand.

26
00:00:58,479 --> 00:01:01,279
So Meera's share is fifty thousand rupees.

27
00:01:02,330 --> 00:01:04,890
Last time, we said it in one sentence.

28
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:07,930
What you have equals what you owe, plus

29
00:01:08,170 --> 00:01:09,130
what's yours.

30
00:01:09,970 --> 00:01:11,730
Today, let's build it.

31
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:14,690
Meet the taraazu — a balance scale.

32
00:01:15,270 --> 00:01:17,830
On the left pan go the assets — everything

33
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:19,030
the stall has.

34
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:22,170
On the right pan go liabilities and equity

35
00:01:22,570 --> 00:01:24,810
— everyone with a claim on those assets.

36
00:01:25,376 --> 00:01:26,016
Watch.

37
00:01:26,256 --> 00:01:28,336
Meera puts in fifty thousand.

38
00:01:28,456 --> 00:01:31,136
Cash on the left, her equity on the right.

39
00:01:31,216 --> 00:01:31,896
Level.

40
00:01:32,776 --> 00:01:35,016
Ravi Mama lends thirty thousand.

41
00:01:35,056 --> 00:01:37,256
The left grows to eighty thousand, and his

42
00:01:37,336 --> 00:01:38,856
loan lands on the right.

43
00:01:38,936 --> 00:01:39,816
Level again.

44
00:01:40,856 --> 00:01:43,416
Eighty thousand equals thirty thousand plus

45
00:01:43,496 --> 00:01:44,616
fifty thousand.

46
00:01:44,696 --> 00:01:46,776
This is the accounting equation.

47
00:01:47,616 --> 00:01:50,336
Assets equal liabilities plus equity.

48
00:01:51,585 --> 00:01:53,265
Now, back to the cart.

49
00:01:53,425 --> 00:01:55,905
Thirty-six thousand rupees left the galla.

50
00:01:55,985 --> 00:01:57,665
Did the stall lose money?

51
00:01:58,345 --> 00:02:00,345
Here's a question we'll ask every single

52
00:02:00,425 --> 00:02:01,785
time money moves.

53
00:02:01,865 --> 00:02:03,705
Which two things changed?

54
00:02:05,893 --> 00:02:06,373
One.

55
00:02:06,613 --> 00:02:09,413
Cash went down by thirty-six thousand.

56
00:02:09,573 --> 00:02:10,053
Two.

57
00:02:10,133 --> 00:02:12,693
The stall now has equipment worth thirty-six

58
00:02:12,853 --> 00:02:13,733
thousand.

59
00:02:13,813 --> 00:02:16,133
Both are assets, so both sit on the left

60
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:16,853
pan.

61
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:19,993
One goes down, one goes up — and the scale

62
00:02:20,073 --> 00:02:21,113
doesn't move.

63
00:02:21,193 --> 00:02:22,873
Still eighty thousand.

64
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:24,633
Meera didn't lose money.

65
00:02:25,253 --> 00:02:27,453
She turned cash into a cart.

66
00:02:27,493 --> 00:02:29,293
The stall isn't worth any less.

67
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:31,693
It just holds its money in a different shape.

68
00:02:32,252 --> 00:02:33,612
Here's where people slip.

69
00:02:34,092 --> 00:02:36,852
She spent thirty-six thousand — so that's

70
00:02:36,892 --> 00:02:37,932
an expense!

71
00:02:39,054 --> 00:02:39,574
Nope.

72
00:02:39,774 --> 00:02:42,814
An expense is money that's used up and gone.

73
00:02:43,054 --> 00:02:44,214
The cart isn't gone.

74
00:02:44,414 --> 00:02:46,334
It's standing right there, ready to make

75
00:02:46,494 --> 00:02:47,134
chai.

76
00:02:47,854 --> 00:02:50,374
It will wear out, slowly — and we'll deal

77
00:02:50,454 --> 00:02:52,054
with that at the end of the month.

78
00:02:53,107 --> 00:02:56,707
Same day, Meera buys tea, milk and sugar.

79
00:02:56,787 --> 00:02:59,747
Six thousand rupees of stock, paid in cash.

80
00:02:59,827 --> 00:03:01,427
Which two things changed?

81
00:03:03,507 --> 00:03:05,027
Meera's got the first one.

82
00:03:05,107 --> 00:03:07,347
Stock goes up by six thousand.

83
00:03:07,427 --> 00:03:08,627
Can you name the second?

84
00:03:10,986 --> 00:03:12,986
Cash goes down by six thousand.

85
00:03:13,466 --> 00:03:14,906
Another swap on the left.

86
00:03:15,146 --> 00:03:17,946
The scale stays level, at eighty thousand.

87
00:03:19,089 --> 00:03:20,889
Now you, on your own.

88
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:22,049
April the third.

89
00:03:22,529 --> 00:03:24,769
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

90
00:03:24,849 --> 00:03:27,009
of milk and supplies — the delivery from

91
00:03:27,089 --> 00:03:28,209
last lesson's question.

92
00:03:28,609 --> 00:03:29,889
Meera will pay later.

93
00:03:30,129 --> 00:03:32,369
You already know the two changes: stock

94
00:03:32,449 --> 00:03:34,609
up by eight thousand, and eight thousand

95
00:03:34,769 --> 00:03:35,569
owed to Gopal.

96
00:03:36,049 --> 00:03:38,289
So here's the real question — what does

97
00:03:38,449 --> 00:03:40,449
each side of the scale read now?

98
00:03:43,603 --> 00:03:45,923
Stock goes up by eight thousand, on the

99
00:03:46,083 --> 00:03:46,723
left.

100
00:03:47,203 --> 00:03:50,163
For a moment, the scale tips… then the right

101
00:03:50,243 --> 00:03:51,283
pan catches up.

102
00:03:51,523 --> 00:03:54,483
Meera now owes Gopal eight thousand rupees.

103
00:03:55,203 --> 00:03:57,003
Eighty-eight thousand equals thirty-eight

104
00:03:57,163 --> 00:03:59,163
thousand plus fifty thousand.

105
00:03:59,243 --> 00:03:59,883
Level.

106
00:04:01,132 --> 00:04:02,492
So here's the big idea.

107
00:04:03,052 --> 00:04:05,372
Every transaction has two effects.

108
00:04:06,212 --> 00:04:08,932
Every rupee comes from somewhere, and goes

109
00:04:09,012 --> 00:04:09,652
somewhere.

110
00:04:09,892 --> 00:04:12,292
Accountants call this the dual aspect.

111
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:15,820
And it only ever plays out in four ways.

112
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:19,380
Both sides go up — like Ravi Mama's loan.

113
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:22,540
Both sides go down — like paying off a bill.

114
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:25,940
A swap on the left — cash turns into a cart.

115
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:28,660
Or a swap on the right — one claim turns

116
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:29,500
into another.

117
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:31,180
You'll meet this one in May.

118
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:35,540
In every case, the scale never stays tipped.

119
00:04:36,797 --> 00:04:37,197
So.

120
00:04:37,517 --> 00:04:40,557
The scale: assets on the left, liabilities

121
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:42,237
plus equity on the right.

122
00:04:42,717 --> 00:04:44,917
Every transaction has two effects.

123
00:04:45,117 --> 00:04:47,997
And all four patterns keep the scale level.

124
00:04:49,131 --> 00:04:49,851
Your turn.

125
00:04:50,011 --> 00:04:51,011
Three quick ones.

126
00:04:51,611 --> 00:04:52,131
One.

127
00:04:52,411 --> 00:04:54,571
What does the equation read after Gopal's

128
00:04:54,651 --> 00:04:55,411
delivery?

129
00:04:57,491 --> 00:04:58,051
Two.

130
00:04:58,291 --> 00:05:00,171
Meera buys a cart for cash.

131
00:05:00,371 --> 00:05:01,611
Does the scale move?

132
00:05:03,691 --> 00:05:04,251
Three.

133
00:05:04,491 --> 00:05:07,211
Meera pays back part of Ravi Mama's loan.

134
00:05:07,291 --> 00:05:08,971
Which of the four patterns is that?

135
00:05:11,011 --> 00:05:12,691
Answers next lesson.

136
00:05:14,147 --> 00:05:16,061
And now, Checkpoint One.

137
00:05:16,261 --> 00:05:17,741
A brand-new business.

138
00:05:17,941 --> 00:05:19,781
This is Aman, Meera's friend.

139
00:05:19,861 --> 00:05:21,741
He's starting a samosa cart.

140
00:05:22,521 --> 00:05:23,041
One.

141
00:05:23,321 --> 00:05:25,401
Aman puts in twenty thousand rupees of his

142
00:05:25,481 --> 00:05:26,201
savings.

143
00:05:26,441 --> 00:05:26,881
Two.

144
00:05:27,081 --> 00:05:29,481
He takes a bank loan of ten thousand.

145
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:30,201
Three.

146
00:05:30,281 --> 00:05:33,001
He buys a cart and fryer for eighteen thousand,

147
00:05:33,081 --> 00:05:34,121
in cash.

148
00:05:34,201 --> 00:05:34,841
Four.

149
00:05:34,921 --> 00:05:37,081
He buys three thousand rupees of potatoes,

150
00:05:37,161 --> 00:05:39,721
flour and oil from Sharma Kirana, and he'll

151
00:05:39,801 --> 00:05:40,761
pay later.

152
00:05:41,441 --> 00:05:44,321
For each one: which two things changed?

153
00:05:45,001 --> 00:05:47,201
And what does Aman's scale read after it?

154
00:05:47,601 --> 00:05:48,881
Pause the video now.

155
00:05:48,921 --> 00:05:51,001
The worksheet is linked in the description.

156
00:05:54,158 --> 00:05:54,678
Done?

157
00:05:55,478 --> 00:05:56,318
Let's check.

158
00:05:56,598 --> 00:05:57,078
One.

159
00:05:57,318 --> 00:05:59,918
Aman puts in twenty thousand of his savings.

160
00:06:00,118 --> 00:06:01,718
Cash up, capital up.

161
00:06:01,798 --> 00:06:04,198
Twenty thousand equals zero, plus twenty

162
00:06:04,278 --> 00:06:05,158
thousand.

163
00:06:05,778 --> 00:06:06,298
Two.

164
00:06:06,378 --> 00:06:08,578
He takes a ten-thousand-rupee bank loan.

165
00:06:08,778 --> 00:06:10,698
Cash up, liability up.

166
00:06:10,818 --> 00:06:13,098
Thirty thousand equals ten thousand, plus

167
00:06:13,178 --> 00:06:14,298
twenty thousand.

168
00:06:14,958 --> 00:06:15,518
Three.

169
00:06:15,598 --> 00:06:18,238
He buys a cart and fryer for eighteen thousand,

170
00:06:18,278 --> 00:06:19,118
in cash.

171
00:06:19,158 --> 00:06:21,038
A swap on the left, so the totals don't

172
00:06:21,118 --> 00:06:21,718
change.

173
00:06:22,498 --> 00:06:23,218
Four.

174
00:06:23,298 --> 00:06:25,378
Three thousand rupees of potatoes, flour

175
00:06:25,458 --> 00:06:28,058
and oil, on credit from Sharma Kirana.

176
00:06:28,258 --> 00:06:30,818
Stock up, and what he owes goes up.

177
00:06:30,898 --> 00:06:33,298
Thirty-three thousand equals thirteen thousand,

178
00:06:33,458 --> 00:06:34,738
plus twenty thousand.

179
00:06:34,818 --> 00:06:35,938
Four out of four?

180
00:06:36,618 --> 00:06:38,458
Your scale never tipped either.

181
00:06:40,159 --> 00:06:41,039
Next time.

182
00:06:41,279 --> 00:06:42,359
April the fifth.

183
00:06:42,559 --> 00:06:43,359
Rent day.

184
00:06:43,599 --> 00:06:45,999
Five thousand rupees leave the galla… and

185
00:06:46,159 --> 00:06:47,479
nothing comes back.

186
00:06:47,759 --> 00:06:49,519
Will the scale finally tip?
